+# Summary
+
+**Changed:** implemented `satisfies()` and `max_satisfying()` in `depres/ranges.py` per `docs/RANGES.md` (comparator sets with `||`, primitives, X-ranges/partials, hyphen, tilde, caret, prerelease rule, `ValueError` on invalid range or version). No other files besides this summary were touched; tests unchanged.
+
+**Verified:**
+- `python -m unittest` → 8 tests, all OK (previously all errored).
+- Ad-hoc script (not committed) checking ~30 cases taken from the doc tables/examples (partial-operator table, hyphen partials, all caret/tilde forms, prerelease examples, whitespace after operators, `v` prefix, build metadata, empty/`*` ranges) → all matched the doc. Invalid ranges (`1.x.3`, `1.2.3 -`, `^`, `~>1`, `1.2.3 - 2 - 3`, `>=1.2.3-`) and an invalid version raised `ValueError`.
+
+**Assumptions / not verified:**
+- The doc doesn't specify `>*`/`<*`; I made them match nothing (npm behavior). `>=*`, `=*`, `*` match everything.
+- `max_satisfying` returns the first item when two entries compare equal (e.g. differ only by build metadata), and returns the string as given (`v1.5.0` stays `v1.5.0`).
+- Hyphen ranges are only accepted as a whole comparator set (`A - B`), with no operators on either side.
+- Prerelease tags on partials (`1.2-beta`) are rejected as invalid.
+- No tests beyond the existing file were added.
